  "pd": {
    "basis": "author-died-pre-1930",
    "detail": "Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky died in 1893. Swan Lake Op. 20 was composed in 1875-76 and first staged at the Bolshoi in 1877, so the composition is free worldwide.",
    "sourceEdition": "Tchaikovsky, Swan Lake Op. 20, No. 10 Scene (Moderato); the oboe theme checked note-for-note against a public-domain melodic transcription of the orchestral score in the original key of B minor, 4/4",
    "notes": "Melody and harmony only. This is the original 1876 ballet theme, not any later ballet-suite or film arrangement of it."
  },
  "key": "Bm",
  "mode": "minor",
  "meter": "4/4",
  "tempoBPM": 80,
  "tempoRange": [
    60,
    104
  ],
  "feel": "straight",
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "Form as Tchaikovsky writes it: an eight-bar theme, then an eight-bar rising sequence stated twice, the second time peaking on the Neapolitan -- the melody's flat-2 bar, which spells a literal C major triad and is harmonised as one. The single dominant link bar between the sequence and the reprise is left out so every section stays a clean eight bars. The outro is an arrangement device, not extra Tchaikovsky: it is built only from the theme's own falling 4-3-2 tail over a dying tonic. Harmony is bar-granular, which is what the original mostly does under this tune.",
